    {
      "note": "שְׁפָיִים is an adverbial accusative, so the supplied from and the belong to it (rules 33 and 29). Head on bare: by rule 31's subtraction, heights alone is any heights, exactly as place alone is any place in בָּמָה → high place, so the attributive is what carries the lexeme of a bare height. This is also how the chapter heads the same construction elsewhere, נִיר → fallow ground in 4.3 and כַּרְמֶל → fruitful field in 4.26. The competing reading calls bare and heights a genuine tie and breaks it onto the English noun under rule 24, on the תַּנִּין → sea monsters precedent; that is what this unit previously took, and it is rejected here because rule 31 warns expressly against moving a head onto a generic noun merely because it is the noun.",
